window.docContents[184].resolve({"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Length":{"contents":"\n\n\n\ndef length (l : NatList) : Nat :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => 0\n  | _ :: t => (length t) + 1\n\n\nSome simple facts about list lengths:\n\ntheorem length_nil : [].length = 0 := by rfl\n\ntheorem length_cons (n : Nat) (l : NatList) :\n  (n :: l).length = l.length + 1 := by rfl\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Lists: Working with Structured Data\u0009Lists of Numbers","header":"5.2.2. Length","id":"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Length"},"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Tactics--injection--and--contradiction--Disjointness":{"contents":"\n\nTwo terms beginning with different constructors (like\n0 and Nat.succ, or true and false) can never be equal.\n\n\n\nThe contradiction tactic embodies this principle. If the context\ncontains a contradictory hypothesis, such as false = true,\ncontradiction solves the current goal immediately. Some examples:\n\nexample (n m : Nat)\n    (h : false = true) :\n    n = m := by\n  contradiction\n\nexample (n : Nat)\n    (h : n + 1 = 0) :\n    2 + 2 = 5 := by\n  contradiction\n\n\nThese examples are instances of a logical principle known as the\nprinciple of explosion, which asserts that a contradictory\nhypothesis entails anything (even manifestly false things!).\n\nIs there a way to relate this to the interpretation of implication\nP -> Q where it is true when P is false and Q is true? It seems like\nmaybe this is a computational interpretation so perhaps not.\n\n\n\nSometimes you\nneed to do a little work to expose a contradictory hypothesis involving\nconstructors.\n\n\n\nexample (n : Nat)\n    (h : 1 + n = 0) :\n    2 + 2 = 5 := by\n  contradiction -- doesn't work because `1 + n` doesn't reduce to `n.succ`.\n\n\nTo fix it, rewriting with Nat.one_add changes the\nhypothesis from 1 + n = 0 to n.succ = 0.\nThen Lean can immediately recognize this as impossible.\n\nexample (n : Nat)\n    (h : 1 + n = 0) :\n    2 + 2 = 5 := by\n  rw [Nat.one_add] at h\n  contradiction\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics\u0009Tactics  injection  and  contradiction","header":"7.1.2. Disjointness","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Tactics--injection--and--contradiction--Disjointness"}});